Contents

The Census of Population and Housing is a detailed statistical exercise featuring Malta and its people 
by their demographic and social characteristics. It provides a portrait at a point in time, and for the 
18th Census, this was 21 November 2021. The Census’s legal basis is the national Census Act 1948 
and the supranational EU Regulation No 763/2008. 
Compilation work went into the early months of 2022 and the Preliminary Report was published 
in Summer 2022. Following this, the NSO is publishing the first in a series of thematic Census 
publications. It covers a range of social subjects such as population, migration, citizenship, racial 
origin, and religious affiliation. This introduction synthesises the main findings under these headings.
Introduction
MR ETIENNE CARUANA
Census Officer
Population
• Improved access to administrative registers, and enhanced methods in data collection, allowed the Census Office to arrive
at the final population count in time to publish it in the Preliminary Report.
• The population headcount recorded the highest ever intercensal change, 25 per cent over the Census headcount recorded
in 2011.
• The proportion of males was bigger than that of females for the first time ever - 52 per cent of the population.
• Population density, already the highest in the EU, continued to climb in line with the increase in population.
• The average age of Gozo residents was two years more than the Malta counterpart. As a demographic indicator, the average
age in the Islands was impacted by the influx of foreigners, since foreign nationals generally tend to be younger than the
Maltese.
Immigration
• The number of foreign nationals increased fivefold compared to the last Census in 2011.
• 22.2 per cent of the population were foreign nationals, a steep rise from the 4.9 per cent share recorded in 2011.
• Male foreign nationals outnumbered their female counterparts by more than 20,000.
• Nearly 15,000 incoming migrants lived in another country one year before the Census. This number included an element of 
Maltese returning migrants.
• The foreign community was mostly concentrated in San Pawl il-Baħar, Tas-Sliema and Msida.
Racial origin
Racial origin is a complex concept. People can have single or multiple racial origin. It should not be equated with citizenship, 
language, or religion. Moreover, many factors can influence respondents such as their own perceptions of where they came 
from, as well as their knowledge of their own families’ histories. Among foreign nationals, the proportions were:
• 58.1 per cent Caucasian.
• 22.2 per cent Asian.
• 6.3 per cent African
• 6.0 per cent Arab.
• 4.5 per cent Hispanic or Latino.
Religious affiliation
The question on religion/religious denomination was asked to respondents aged 15 and over. Roman Catholicism was 
predominant, with emerging shares of Islam and Orthodoxy:
• 82.6 per cent belonged to or identified themselves with Roman Catholicism.
• 3.9 per cent belonged to or identified themselves with Islam.
• 3.6 per cent belonged to or identified themselves with Orthodoxy.
Acknowledgements
The above are only some excerpts from the rich and diverse fund of Census findings. I am sure that those users who choose to 
delve deep will find ample material to plan their own analysis and customise their own texts and tables. My thanks are due to 
the Census Office within the NSO which compiled the tables and undertook the analysis, led by Silvan Zammit and Matthew 
Zerafa, the two Deputy Census Officers. A special mention is due to the public entities, organisations and NGOs which made 
available their administrative registers, and which gave advice. The ultimate acknowledgement must be for the contribution of 
the resident population of Malta whose response formed the backbone of the Census results.
