Estimate Census
On Census Day, there were 480,275 persons residing in Malta and 39,287 residing in Gozo. Since the 2011 Census, both regions 
registered an increase of approximately 25 per cent.  The population likewise grew in all the six geographical districts, though 
this growth was not equal.  
The highest population growth was observed in the Northern district with an increase of 47 per cent compared to 2011 (a gain 
of approximately 30,000 residents).  The second largest increase was noted in the Northern Harbour district (30.6 per cent).  On 
the other hand, the district with the smallest growth was the Southern Harbour district, recording an increase of 8.3 per cent.
The three largest localities are San Pawl il-Baħar, Birkirkara and Il-Mosta – these three localities alone account for 15.7 per cent of 
the total population in 2021.  The population in San Pawl il-Baħar has almost doubled since 2011, exceeding 32,000 inhabitants.
The localities registering the largest increases in population in 2021 were San Pawl Il-Baħar (95.4 per cent), Iż-Żebbuġ, Għawdex 
(79.4 per cent) and L-Imsida (75.4 per cent).  These are all localities which are predominantly characterised by a high share of 
foreign residents.  Eight localities had a decrease in population since 2011, and six of these were localities within the Southern 
Harbour district.
Population density
Malta remains the most densely populated country in the EU with 1,649 residents per square kilometre.  The average population 
density in the EU, in 2019, stood at 109 persons per square kilometre.  While the Netherlands is the second most densely 
populated country, it is still three times less dense than Malta, with 507 persons per square kilometre.
Differences in population density within Malta exist when comparing across varying geographic levels.  On a regional level, the 
population density in Malta stood at 1,948 persons per square kilometre, compared to 572 inhabitants per square kilometre in 
Gozo.
15
The Northern Harbour district is the densest district, with 6,547 residents per square kilometre.  Localities within this district, 
like Tas-Sliema, Tal-Pieta’ and Il-Gżira, have a population density of more than 10,000 persons per square kilometre. Similar 
thresholds were recorded among localities within the Southern Harbour district.  Localities like Ħal Tarxien, Il-Fgura and L-Isla, 
also recorded a population density of more than 10,000 persons per square kilometre.
