Tas-Sliema is the most densely populated locality with 15,167 persons per square kilometre while L-Għasri is the least with 104 
persons per square kilometre - the latter being the only locality which is comparable to the EU average.  Among Gozitan localities, 
Ir-Rabat, Għawdex and Il-Fontana had the largest population densities with more than 2,000 inhabitants per square kilometre in 
each locality.
Sex
There were 270,021 men (52 per cent of the population) and 249,541 women in Malta, resulting in a sex ratio of 924 females per 
1,000 males.  For the first time ever, the 2021 Census recorded more males than females within the Maltese population.  This 
resulted from a faster growth among the male population (+ 30.1 per cent) over the past 10 years when compared to the female 
counterparts (+18.9 per cent). This is different from the sex distribution in the EU (as at January 2021) where there are more 
females (51.1 per cent) than males.  In percentage terms, among EU countries with a higher share of males, Malta had the largest 
percentage of male residents.  This was followed by Luxembourg with 50.3 per cent of the population being males.   
Similar sex distributions are observed in Malta and Gozo.  At district level, the Western district had an almost even share of males 
(50.6 per cent) and females (49.4 per cent); and the South Eastern district recorded the widest gap between the sexes (52.5 per 
cent being males and 47.5 per cent being females).
